      "source_number": "750",
      "tasks": [
        "Construct the graph of y=x^2 and identify its increasing/decreasing input sets.",
        "Construct the graph of y=-x^2 and identify its increasing/decreasing input sets."
      ],
      "solution": "For y=x^2, the points at x=-2,-1,0,1,2 are (-2,4),(-1,1),(0,0),(1,1),(2,4); draw the smooth upward-opening parabola with vertex(0,0). For y=-x^2, reflect these points in the x-axis to obtain (-2,-4),(-1,-1),(0,0),(1,-1),(2,-4); draw the downward-opening parabola. Algebraically x2^2-x1^2=(x2-x1)(x2+x1). For x1<x2<0 the first factor is positive and the second negative, so x^2 decreases; for0<x1<x2 both are positive, so x^2 increases. Negation reverses these inequalities. Thus x^2 decreases on ]-infinity,0[ and increases on ]0,+infinity[; -x^2 increases on ]-infinity,0[ and decreases on ]0,+infinity[. Neither is strictly monotone on all R. Zero is defined and is the vertex; excluding it from displayed intervals follows the lesson's reporting style, not a domain exclusion.",
