      "source_number": "751",
      "tasks": [
        "Construct the graph of the corrected y=-6/x and identify its increasing/decreasing intervals.",
        "Construct the graph of y=4/x and identify its increasing/decreasing intervals."
      ],
      "solution": "Both domains are R excluding0. For y=k/x and x2>x1 within one same-sign branch, f(x2)-f(x1)=-k(x2-x1)/(x1*x2), and x1*x2>0. Therefore y=-6/x increases separately on ]-infinity,0[ and ]0,+infinity[. Its branches are in quadrantsII andIV; points (-6,1),(-3,2),(-2,3),(-1,6),(1,-6),(2,-3),(3,-2),(6,-1) locate the graph, which approaches but never meets either axis. For y=4/x the difference is negative, so it decreases separately on these two intervals. Its branches are in quadrantsI andIII; points (-4,-1),(-2,-2),(-1,-4),(1,4),(2,2),(4,1) locate it, with the same axes as asymptotes. Neither function is globally strictly increasing or decreasing on its disconnected whole domain. For -6/x, crossing from x=-1 to x=1 takes y from6 to-6, contradicting global increase, while same-branch increase contradicts global decrease. For4/x, the same crossing takes y from-4 to4, contradicting global decrease, while same-branch decrease contradicts global increase. There is no decreasing nondegenerate branch interval for -6/x and no increasing nondegenerate branch interval for4/x.",
